Notice that the two fractions have the same denominator, so we can add them. Addition is commutative, so we can rearrange the terms to get  \begin{align*}
\frac{k-3}{2} +\frac{3k+1}{2}+3k+1 &=\frac{4k-2}{2}+3k+1 \\
&=2k-1+3k+1 \\
&=\boxed{5k}.\end{align*}

prediction:
The matrix for reflecting through a plane $P$ is given by $I-2\mathbf{n}\mathbf{n}^T$, where $\mathbf{n}$ is the normal vector of $P$.  Therefore, we have
\begin{align*}
\begin{pmatrix}
\frac{11}{15} & \frac{2}{15} & \frac{2}{3} \\
\frac{2}{15} & \frac{14}{15} & -\frac{1}{3} \\
\frac{2}{3} & -\frac{1}{3} & -\frac{2}{3}
\end{pmatrix}
&=I-2\begin{pmatrix}
a \\ b \\ c
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
a & b & c
\end{pmatrix}\\
&=\begin{pmatrix}
1-2a^2 & -2ab & -2ac \\
-2ab & 1-2b^2 & 2bc \\
-2ac & 2bc & 1-2c^2
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}Equating the $(1,1)$-entry of both sides, we have
\begin{align*}
\frac{11}{15}&=1-2a^2\\
\Rightarrow\qquad a^2&=\frac{4}{15}\\
\Rightarrow\qquad a&=\pm\frac{2}{\sqrt{15}}=\pm\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}.
\end{align*}Since $a>0$, we have $a=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}$.  Similarly, we can find $b$ and $c$:
\begin{align*}
\frac{2}{15}&=-2ab=-2\cdot\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}\cdot\frac{b}{\sqrt{3}}=-\frac{2}{3}b\\
\Rightarrow\qquad b&=-\frac{3}{4},\\
-\frac{2}{3}&=-2ac=-2\cdot\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}\cdot\frac{c}{\sqrt{3}}=-\frac{2}{3}c\\
\Rightarrow\qquad c&=1.
\end{align*}Therefore, the normal vector of $P$ is $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix}\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}\\-\frac{3}{4}\\1\end{pmatrix}}$.
Final Answer: The final answer is \begin{pmatrix}\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}\\-\frac{3}{4}\\1\end{pmatrix}. I hope it is correct.
